course: dictionary
lesson: 2393
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Current is one of them. You can tell which meaning is meant by reading the words around it, which is the context. «Lifeguards move the flags during the day, because the safe place changes as the tide and currents change.» Here, currents means moving water in the sea.
explain: «A telephone turns sound into a changing electric current and turns it back into sound at the other end.» Here, current means the flow of electricity through a wire, not water. Ask yourself: is this sentence about water, electricity or time? That question helps you pick the right meaning.
explain: Current can also be an adjective meaning happening or existing now. «Current news» means up-to-date news. Try swapping in «now» or «up to date». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A current is a strong movement of water or air in one direction.
fact: An electric current is the flow of electricity through a wire.
fact: «A rip current is a strong current that flows away from the shore.»
fact: As an adjective, «current» means happening or existing now, as in «current events».
fact: «Currently» means at the present time.
exercise: «Never swim near dams or reservoirs; the water is cold and deep, with hidden currents.» What are hidden currents? | Lights that glow at night | *Strong movements of water under the surface | Old news stories | Electric wires hidden inside the walls
exercise: «A telephone turns sound into a changing electric current.» Which meaning of current is this? | *The flow of electricity through a wire | Happening now | A strong movement of water | A kind of small boat
exercise: Find and copy the word that tells you what a rip current does to you: «If a rip current carries you, you should float, signal for help or swim parallel to the shore.» | float | signal | *carries | shore
test: «Lifeguards move the flags because the safe place changes as the tide and currents change.» In this sentence, currents means: | A type of shoe | *Moving water in the sea | Happening right now | Electricity flowing in a wire
test: Mum says, «The current weather is sunny.» What does current mean here? | Strong water moving out to sea | Very old | Flowing through a wire | *Happening right now
exam: Maya says current can only mean water. Is she right? | Yes, because current is a noun and nouns only mean water | No, current means very old | Yes, current always means moving water | *No, current can also mean electricity or happening now, so you must check the context
